.TH "HED-SED" "1" "2026-08-11" "hed v1.19.0" "hed manual"
.SH NAME
hed\-sed \- :sed  pipes the active buffer through an external sed
process and replaces the buffer with the output.
.PP
\f[CR]:sed <expression>\f[R] pipes the active buffer through an external
\f[CR]sed\f[R] process and replaces the buffer with the output.
.SH Examples
.IP
.EX
:sed s/foo/bar/g       # global substitution
:sed /\[ha]#/d             # delete comment lines
:sed 1,10d             # delete first ten lines
:sed \[aq]s/\[ha]/    /\[aq]       # indent every line by 4 spaces
:sed \-E \[aq]s/[0\-9]+/N/g\[aq] # extended regex
.EE
.PP
The expression is passed to \f[CR]sed \-e <expr>\f[R], so escape shell
metacharacters as you would on the command line.
.SH Behavior
.IP \[bu] 2
Reads the buffer\[cq]s current contents (in memory, not from disk).
.IP \[bu] 2
Pipes them to \f[CR]sed\f[R] on stdin.
.IP \[bu] 2
Reads \f[CR]sed\f[R]\[cq]s stdout and replaces the buffer atomically.
.IP \[bu] 2
Bumps the dirty flag on success \[em] undo reverts the change in one
step.
.IP \[bu] 2
Cursor position is preserved (clamped to the new line count if the
buffer shrank).
.SH Requirements
The system \f[CR]sed\f[R] binary on \f[CR]$PATH\f[R].
GNU sed and BSD sed both work.
.SH Limitations
.IP \[bu] 2
The whole buffer is replaced \[em] no range support yet
(\f[CR]:5,10sed   ...\f[R] is on the wishlist).
.IP \[bu] 2
Read\-only buffers refuse the substitution.
.IP \[bu] 2
Very large buffers (>10 MB) may be slow because the whole buffer is
serialized through the pipe.
.SH Underlying API
Other plugins can reuse the mechanic:
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]\[dq]sed/sed.h\[dq]\f[R]

EdError sed_apply_to_buffer(Buffer *buf, const char *sed_expr);
.EE
.PP
Returns \f[CR]ED_OK\f[R] on success, or one of
\f[CR]ED_ERR_INVALID_ARG\f[R], \f[CR]ED_ERR_BUFFER_READONLY\f[R],
\f[CR]ED_ERR_NOMEM\f[R], \f[CR]ED_ERR_SYSTEM\f[R].
Status message is set on error.
